# Targeting versus "each" (CR 702.96a): Bot2's True-Name Nemesis has protection from Bot1, so a Damn naming it as its target is
# refused (the refusal, beside the same Damn cast on the Ornithopter, the positive control). Overloaded, Damn has no target,
# and protection does not stop destruction (CR 702.16: damage, enchanting and equipping, blocking and targeting only), so the second
# Damn, overloaded, destroys the Nemesis. Bot1 pays for both with four Moxen and two Plains on turn three.

[Bot1 Deck]
Mox Jet
Mox Jet
Mox Pearl
Mox Pearl
Damn
Damn
Plains
Plains
Plains x10

[Bot2 Deck]
Mox Sapphire
Mox Sapphire
Mox Sapphire
True-Name Nemesis
Ornithopter
Island x10

[Actions]
Bot1.Hand.Mox Jet.Cast
Bot1.Hand.Mox Jet.Cast
Bot1.Hand.Mox Pearl.Cast
Bot1.Hand.Mox Pearl.Cast
Bot1.Hand.Plains.Play
[T2 Bot2.Main1]
Bot2.Hand.Mox Sapphire.Cast
Bot2.Hand.Mox Sapphire.Cast
Bot2.Hand.Mox Sapphire.Cast
Bot2.Battlefield.Mox Sapphire#1.Activate
Bot2.Battlefield.Mox Sapphire#2.Activate
Bot2.Battlefield.Mox Sapphire#3.Activate
Bot2.Hand.True-Name Nemesis.Cast
Bot2.ChoosePlayer(Bot1)
Bot2.Hand.Ornithopter.Cast
[T3 Bot1.Main1]
Bot1.Hand.Plains.Play
! Bot1.Hand.Damn.Cast(Bot2.Battlefield.True-Name Nemesis)
Bot1.Battlefield.Mox Jet#1.Activate
Bot1.Battlefield.Mox Jet#2.Activate
Bot1.Hand.Damn.Cast(Bot2.Battlefield.Ornithopter)
[T3 Bot1.Main2]
Bot1.Battlefield.Mox Pearl#1.Activate
Bot1.Battlefield.Mox Pearl#2.Activate
Bot1.Battlefield.Plains#1.Activate
Bot1.Battlefield.Plains#2.Activate
Bot1.Hand.Damn.Cast(Ability: Overload)
[T3 Bot1.End]

[Expect]
Bot2.Graveyard = True-Name Nemesis; Ornithopter
Bot2.Battlefield.Count = 3
Bot1.Graveyard = Damn; Damn
